    std::cout << "Client: Testing client code with the first factory type:
";
    ConcreteFactory1 f1;
    ClientCode(f1);
    return 0;
}

lessons.lessonContent:
Structural design patterns deal with object composition and relationships, providing flexible ways to assemble objects to form larger structures. This lesson covers three advanced structural patterns: Adapter, Decorator, and Bridge.

**1. Adapter:**

The Adapter pattern allows objects with incompatible interfaces to collaborate. It acts as a bridge between two incompatible interfaces, translating the requests from one interface to another.

**Analogy:** Think of an electrical adapter. It allows you to plug a device with one type of plug into a socket with a different type of connector.

**Example:**
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

// Target interface
class Target {
public:
    virtual ~Target() = default;
    virtual std::string Request() const {
        return "Target: The default target's behavior.";
    }
};

// Adaptee class (incompatible interface)
class Adaptee {
public:
    std::string SpecificRequest() const {
        return ".eetpadA eht fo roivaheb cificepS";
    }
};

// Adapter class
class Adapter : public Target {
public:
    Adapter(Adaptee* adaptee) : adaptee_(adaptee) {}
    std::string Request() const override {
        std::string to_reverse = adaptee_->SpecificRequest();
        std::reverse(to_reverse.begin(), to_reverse.end());
        return "Adapter: (TRANSLATED) " + to_reverse;
    }
private:
    Adaptee* adaptee_;
};

// Client code
void ClientCode(const Target* target) {
    std::cout << target->Request();
}

int main() {
    std::cout << "Client: I can work just fine with the Target objects:\n";
    Target* target = new Target;
    ClientCode(target);
    std::cout << "\n\n";
    Adaptee* adaptee = new Adaptee;
    std::cout << "Client: The Adaptee class has a weird interface. See, I don't understand it:\n";
    std::cout << "Adaptee: " << adaptee->SpecificRequest() << "\n\n";
    std::cout << "Client: But I can work with it via the Adapter:\n";
    Adapter* adapter = new Adapter(adaptee);
    ClientCode(adapter);
    std::cout << "\n";

    delete target;
    delete adaptee;
    delete adapter;

    return 0;
}
```

**2. Decorator:**

The Decorator pattern allows you to add responsibilities to an object dynamically without modifying its structure. It provides a flexible alternative to subclassing for extending functionality.

**Analogy:** Think of decorating a Christmas tree. You can add different decorations (lights, ornaments, tinsel) to the tree without changing the tree itself.

**Example:**

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

// Component interface
class Component {
public:
    virtual ~Component() = default;
    virtual std::string Operation() const = 0;
};

// Concrete Component
class ConcreteComponent : public Component {
public:
    std::string Operation() const override {
        return "ConcreteComponent";
    }
};

// Base Decorator class
class Decorator : public Component {
public:
    Decorator(Component* component) : component_(component) {}
    std::string Operation() const override {
        if(component_) { 
             return component_->Operation();
        } else { 
            return "";
        }
    }
protected:
    Component* component_;
};

// Concrete Decorator A
class ConcreteDecoratorA : public Decorator {
public:
    ConcreteDecoratorA(Component* component) : Decorator(component) {}
    std::string Operation() const override {
        return "ConcreteDecoratorA(" + Decorator::Operation() + ")";
    }
};

// Concrete Decorator B
class ConcreteDecoratorB : public Decorator {
public:
    ConcreteDecoratorB(Component* component) : Decorator(component) {}
    std::string Operation() const override {
        return "ConcreteDecoratorB(" + Decorator::Operation() + ")";
    }
};

// Client code
void ClientCode(Component* component) {
    std::cout << "RESULT: " << component->Operation();
}

int main() {
    Component* simple = new ConcreteComponent;
    std::cout << "Client: I've got a simple component:\n";
    ClientCode(simple);
    std::cout << "\n\n";
    Component* decorator1 = new ConcreteDecoratorA(simple);
    Component* decorator2 = new ConcreteDecoratorB(decorator1);
    std::cout << "Client: Now I've got a decorated component:\n";
    ClientCode(decorator2);
    std::cout << "\n";

    delete simple;
    delete decorator1;
    delete decorator2;

    return 0;
}
```

**3. Bridge:**

The Bridge pattern decouples an abstraction from its implementation so that the two can vary independently. It avoids tight coupling between the abstraction and its implementation, allowing you to change them separately.

**Analogy:** Think of a TV remote control. The remote (abstraction) is separate from the TV (implementation). You can use the same remote to control different TVs, and you can use different remotes to control the same TV.

**Best Practices:**

*   Use Adapter when you need to make existing classes work with others without modifying their source code.
*   Use Decorator when you need to add responsibilities to individual objects dynamically and transparently.
*   Use Bridge when you need to decouple an abstraction from its implementation so that the two can vary independently.

**Real-World Scenarios:**
*   Adapter: Integrating a legacy system with a new system.
*   Decorator: Adding logging or caching to a service.
*   Bridge: Creating a platform-independent GUI framework.

Behavioral design patterns focus on communication and assignment of responsibilities between objects. They help in managing complex interactions and defining algorithms in a flexible and reusable way. This lesson covers three advanced behavioral patterns: Observer, Strategy, and Command.

**1. Observer:**

The Observer pattern defines a one-to-many dependency between objects so that when one object changes state, all its dependents are notified and updated automatically. It promotes loose coupling between the subject (the object being observed) and the observers (the objects that are notified).

**Analogy:** Think of a newspaper subscription. The newspaper (subject) publishes new articles, and the subscribers (observers) are notified when new content is available.

**Example:**

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>

// Abstract Observer
class Observer {
public:
    virtual ~Observer() = default;
    virtual void Update(const std::string& message_from_subject) = 0;
};

// Concrete Observer
class ConcreteObserverA : public Observer {
public:
    void Update(const std::string& message_from_subject) override {
        message_ = message_from_subject;
        std::cout << "ConcreteObserverA received message: " << message_ << "\n";
    }
private:
    std::string message_;
};

class ConcreteObserverB : public Observer {
public:
    void Update(const std::string& message_from_subject) override {
        message_ = message_from_subject;
        std::cout << "ConcreteObserverB received message: " << message_ << "\n";
    }
private:
    std::string message_;
};

// Abstract Subject
class Subject {
public:
    virtual ~Subject() = default;
    virtual void Attach(Observer* observer) {
        list_observer_.push_back(observer);
    }
    virtual void Detach(Observer* observer) {
        list_observer_.remove(observer);
    }
    virtual void Notify(const std::string& message) {
        for (Observer* observer : list_observer_) {
            observer->Update(message);
        }
    }
    virtual void CreateMessage(std::string message = "Empty") {
        this->message_ = message;
        Notify(message_);
    }
private:
    std::list<Observer*> list_observer_;
    std::string message_;
};

// Concrete Subject
class ConcreteSubject : public Subject {
public:
    void CreateMessage(std::string message = "Empty") override {
        this->message_ = message;
        Notify(message_);
    }
private:
    std::string message_;
};

int main() {
    ConcreteSubject* subject = new ConcreteSubject;

    ConcreteObserverA* observer1 = new ConcreteObserverA;
    subject->Attach(observer1);

    ConcreteObserverB* observer2 = new ConcreteObserverB;
    subject->Attach(observer2);

    subject->CreateMessage("Hello, observers!");

    subject->Detach(observer2);

    subject->CreateMessage("Goodbye, observer B!");

    delete observer1;
    delete observer2;
    delete subject;

    return 0;
}
```

**2. Strategy:**

The Strategy pattern defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangeable. Strategy lets the algorithm vary independently from clients that use it.  The client delegates the algorithm to be used to the strategy object.

**Analogy:** Think of different sorting algorithms (e.g., quicksort, mergesort, heapsort). You can choose which sorting algorithm to use based on the specific requirements of the task.

**3. Command:**

The Command pattern encapsulates a request as an object, thereby letting you parameterize clients with different requests, queue or log requests, and support undoable operations.  It decouples the object that invokes the operation from the one that knows how to perform it.

**Analogy:** Think of a restaurant order. The customer (client) places an order (command) with the waiter (invoker), who then passes it on to the chef (receiver) to prepare the meal.

**Best Practices:**

*   Use Observer when you need to maintain consistency between related objects without tightly coupling them.
*   Use Strategy when you need to select an algorithm at runtime and you want to encapsulate the algorithms in separate classes.
*   Use Command when you need to encapsulate requests as objects, support undoable operations, or queue requests.

